  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 239 AM CDT Sat Jul 25 2026\n\nThe latest RAP analysis and satellite imagery show shortwave \nenergy pushing east across south-central Canada into northeast \nNorth Dakota and northern Minnesota, driving a cold front into \nnorthwest Minnesota. Thunderstorm activity ahead of this front has\nbeen weakening across northern Minnesota as it outruns stronger \ninstability, though light showers continue to push into far north-\ncentral Wisconsin early this morning. Further south and west, a \ndominant subtropical ridge centered over the Intermountain West \ncontinues to build northeast toward the northern Plains and Upper \nMidwest, anchored by extreme heat over the Plains. Widespread low \nstratus is lifting northeast across the state in a low-level warm \nadvection regime. Over the next 36 to 48 hours, the interaction \nbetween embedded northern-stream shortwaves propagating along the \nnorthern periphery of this building subtropical ridge and the \nadvancing surface front will serve as the primary driver for \nconvective potential and dangerous heat across the region.\n\nRegarding thunderstorm potential, the decaying surface front will\ntrack east today, losing definition near Lake Superior. Morning \ncloud cover (and persistent light showers) over north-central \nWisconsin should mix out by afternoon, allowing for surface \nheating and destabilization. Mid-afternoon CAPE values reaching \n1000-2000 J/kg alongside lake breeze convergence will favor \nisolated thunderstorm development from 4 PM to 9 PM, primarily \nover far northeast Wisconsin. While weak overall convergence and \ncapping will keep storm coverage sparse, any cell that develops \ncould become conditionally severe, with large hail and damaging \nwinds as the primary threats. A second, more organized round of \nconvection is possible late night into Sunday morning as a 25-30 \nknot low-level jet over Minnesota supports a cluster of storms \nriding elevated instability around 2000 J/kg into north- central \nWisconsin. Effective shear of 50-60 knots could organize these \nearly Sunday storms capable of damaging winds and hail before \ndissipating. Depending on how morning convection modifies the \nairmass, afternoon re-development on Sunday remains conditional \ndue to rising 700mb temperatures near 12C creating a stout cap.\nNortheast Wisconsin remains the most favorable area for\nthunderstorm development on Sunday afternoon, which is an area on\nthe edge of the mid-level cap. A third period of severe potential\narrives Monday as a stronger shortwave trough and cold front \nsweep through the region, interacting with 2000-3000 J/kg CAPE and\ndeep- layer shear exceeding 40 knots. Much of the area is in a\nslight risk of severe thunderstorms on Monday.\n\nRegarding the heat threat, the expanding subtropical ridge will\nusher a hot, muggy airmass into the region on Sunday and Monday. \nAfternoon high temperatures in the upper 80s to lower 90s combined\nwith dew points climbing into the lower 70s will yield heat index\nvalues peaking in the middle to upper 90s on both days, \nparticularly across central and east-central Wisconsin. While \nconvective debris and thunderstorm coverage introduce some \nuncertainty regarding exact peak temperatures, conditions will \nremain dangerously hot and humid for those outdoors. Headlines for\nexcessive heat may be required as confidence in storm coverage \nimproves. A cold front will cross the region late Monday into \nMonday night, shifting winds to the north on Tuesday and \ndelivering a reprieve from the oppressive humidity. Highs Tuesday \nthrough Thursday will moderate into the upper 70s to middle 80s \nwith much lower dew points, before another disturbance brings \nrenewed thunderstorm chances late Thursday into Friday.",
